<section xml:id="sec-ftoc">
<title>The fundamental theorem of calculus</title>
<p>The main reference for the rest of this chapter is <xref ref="Folland"/>, though we present the results in less generality than what is done in <xref ref="Folland"/>. We begin with a result which has nothing at all to do with differentiation. It could have appeared in <xref ref="chap-integration"/>, but we include here, because we need it here.</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-integrable-close-to-cont">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a Lebesgue integrable function on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
            For all <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m> there exists a continuous Lebesgue integrable function <m>g</m> on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> such that
            <md> \int_\mathbb{R} |f-g|\,dm &lt; \varepsilon.</md>            
        </p>
    </statement>

<proof>
<p>We will deal first with the non-negative case.
    Fix <m>\varepsilon &gt;0</m>.
    Since <m>f</m> is integrable,
    <m>\lim_{N\rightarrow \infty} \int_{[-N,N]} f\,dm = \int_\mathbb{R} f\,dm&lt;\infty.</m>
    Hence, there is an <m>N&gt;0</m> such that
<md> \int_\mathbb{R} f\,dm - \int_{[-N,N]}f\,dm&lt;\varepsilon/3.</md>
Suppose <m>g</m> is a non-negative continuous function on <m>[-N,N]</m> such that
<md>\int_{[-N,N]} |f- g|\,dm&lt;\varepsilon/3.</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-integrable-close-to-cont">
    <caption>
    <p>An example of <m>g_n</m> defined in the proof of <xref ref="thm-integrable-close-to-cont"/>.</p>
    </caption>
    <image>
        <shortdescription>Visualization of a positive polynomial g on an interval [-N,N] extended to a function g_n on the entire real line.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
            <p>
                Visualization of a positive polynomial <m>g</m> on an interval <m>[-N,N]</m> extended to a function <m>g_n</m> on all of <m>\bR</m> by drawing straight lines from <m>(-N-1/n,0)</m> to <m>(-N,g(-N))</m> and from <m>(N,g(N))</m> to <m>(N+1/n,0)</m>, and setting <m>g_n(x) = 0</m> outside of <m>(-N-\frac{1}{n},N+\frac{1}{n})</m>.
            </p>
        </description>
        <sageplot label="cont-diff">
            <!-- g(x) = -1/2*sin(1/3*x)+5 -->
            g(x) = 1/3*x^3 +1/20*x^2 - 3/4*x + 10

            N = 1
            n = 3

            plt = plot(g, [-N,N], legend_label="$g_n(x)$", ticks=[[-N-1/n,-N,N,N+1/n],[18]], tick_formatter=[[r"$-N-\frac{1}{n}$","$-N$","$N$",r"$N+\frac{1}{n}$"],""])

            L1 = line([(-N,0),(-N,g(-N))], linestyle="--")
            L2 = line([(N,0),(N,g(N))], linestyle="--")

            cont_L1 = line([(-N-1/n,0),(-N,g(-N))])
            cont_L2 = line([(N+1/n,0),(N,g(N))])

            L3 = line([(-N-3,0),(-N-1/n,0)])
            L4 = line([(N+1/n,0),(N+3,0)])

            plt += L1 + L2 + cont_L1 + cont_L2 + L3 + L4

            plt.set_axes_range(-N-2/n,N+2/n,0,11)
            plt
        </sageplot>
    </image>
</figure>

<p>For each <m>n</m>, we define <m>g_n</m> by <m>g_n(x) = g(x)</m> for <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>; <m>g_n(x) = 0</m> for <m>x \notin (-N-\frac{1}{n},N+\frac{1}{n})</m>; <m>g_n</m> is the straight line from the point <m>(-N-\frac{1}{n},0)</m> to <m>(-N,g(-N))</m> on <m>(-N-\frac{1}{n},-N)</m>; and <m>g_n</m> is the straight line from the point <m>(N,g(N))</m> to <m>(N+\frac{1}{n}),0)</m> on <m>(-N-\frac{1}{n},-N)</m> (see Figure~\ref{fig: integrable close to cont}).
    Choose <m>n\geq 0</m> so that <m>g(N)/n&lt;\varepsilon/6</m> and <m>g(-N)/n&lt;\varepsilon/6</m>.
    Then,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\int_\mathbb{R} |f - g_n|\,dm =&amp; \int_{(-\infty,-N-\frac{1}{n})}f\,dm + \int_{(-N-\frac{1}{n},-N)}|f-g_n|\,dm\\ &amp;+ \int_{[-N,N]}|f-g_n|\,dm+\int_{(N,N+\frac{1}{n})}|f-g_n|\,dm + \int_{(N,\infty)} f\,dm \\
    \leq&amp; \int_{(-\infty,-N)\cup(N,\infty)}f\,dm+\int_{[-N,N]}|f-g|\,dm\\
    &amp;+ \int_{(-N-\frac{1}{n},-N)}g_n\,dm +\int_{(N,N+\frac{1}{n})}g_n\,dm\\
    &lt;&amp; \varepsilon/3 + \varepsilon/3 + \varepsilon/6 + \varepsilon/6 = \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence, to prove the result for <m>f</m>, it suffice to show that the result holds when <m>f</m> is restricted to a closed interval.
    We can thus assume that <m>f</m> is a non-negative integrable function on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>.</p>
<p>By the definition of <m>\int_{[a,b]}f\,dm</m> and by <xref ref="thm-Riemann-is-Lebesgue"/>,
<md> \int_{[a,b]} f\,dm = \sup\left\{ \int_{[a,b]} \varphi\,dm \colon 0\leq \varphi \leq f, \ \varphi \text{ a step function}.\right\}.</md>
Let <m>\varphi</m> be a step function such that <m>0\leq \varphi\leq f</m> and
<md> \int_{[a,b]}f-\varphi\,dm &lt; \varepsilon/2.</md>
Suppose <m>\varphi = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i \chi_{I_i}</m> where <m>\{I_i\}_{i=1}^n</m> are disjoint intervals.
    Without loss of generalization, assume <m>a_i \neq 0</m> for each <m>1\leq i \leq n</m>.
    For each <m>i</m>, let <m>g_i</m> be a continuous function with <m>0\leq g_i \leq \chi_{I_i}</m> such that
<md> \int_\mathbb{R} (\chi_{I_i}- g_i) \,dm &lt; \frac{\varepsilon}{n a_i}.</md>
Let <m>g = \sum_{i=1}^n a g_i</m>.
    Then <m>g</m> is a continuous function with <m>0\leq g \leq \varphi \leq f</m>, and
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\int_{[a,b]}(f-g)\,dm = \int_{[a,b]}(f-\varphi)\,dm - \int_{[a,b]}(\varphi - g)\,dm &lt; \varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>If <m>f</m> is a (not necessarily non-negative) integrable function, then <m>f = f_+ - f_-</m>, where <m>f_+</m> and <m>f_-</m> are the positive and negative parts of <m>f</m>, respectively.
    By the above argument, there are continuous functions <m>g_+</m> and <m>g_-</m> such that <m>0\leq g_+ \leq f_+</m>, <m>0\leq g_-\leq f_-</m>,
<md> \int_\mathbb{R} (f_+ - g_+)\,dm &lt; \varepsilon/2 \text{ and } \int_\mathbb{R} (f_- - g_-)\,dm &lt; \varepsilon/2.</md>
Let <m>g = g_+ - g_-</m>. Then <m>g</m> is a continuous function and
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\int_\mathbb{R} |f-g|\,dm =&amp; \int_\mathbb{R} |f_+-g_+ - f_- + g_-|\,dm\\
        \leq&amp; \int_\mathbb{R}(f_+ - g_+)\,dm+\int_\mathbb{R}(f_- - g_-)\,dm\\
        &lt;&amp;\varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
</proof>
</theorem>


<p>For each <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>r&gt;0</m>, denote by <m>B(x,r)</m> the open interval of length <m>2r</m> centred at <m>x</m>. That is <m>B(x,r) = (x-r,x+r)</m>.<fn>The <m>B</m> in this definition stands for `ball'. That term makes more sense when working in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>, but we will stick with the notation here, even though we are working in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</fn></p>

<definition>
<notation>
    <usage><m> A_rf(x)</m></usage>
  <description>average value function</description>
</notation>

<notation>
    <usage><m>Hf</m></usage>
  <description>Hardy-Littlewood maximal function</description>
</notation>

<statement>
    <p>
        Suppose <m>f</m> is an integrable function on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
        For each <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>r &gt;0</m>, we define <m>A_rf(x)</m> as
        <md> A_rf(x) = \frac{1}{2r}\int_{B(r,x)} f\,dm.</md>
        
        We will call <m>A_rf</m> the <term>average value function</term> for <m>f</m> with radius <m>r</m>.</p>
        <p>Define the <term>Hardy-Littlewood maximal function</term>, <m>Hf</m> for <m>f</m> by
        <md> Hf(x) = \sup_{r&gt;0}A_r|f|(x) = \sup_{r&gt;0} \frac{1}{2r}\int_{\mathbb{R}}|f|\,dm.</md>
        <idx><h>average value function</h></idx>
        <idx><h>Hardy-Littlewood maximal function</h></idx>
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>

<lemma xml:id="lem-avg-cont">
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>f</m> is an integrable function on some closed interval <m>[a,b] \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m>. 
        The function <m>A_rf(x)</m> is jointly continuous in <m>r&gt;0</m> and <m>x</m>.     
    </p>
</statement>

<proof>
<p>Fix <m>r_0&gt;0</m> and <m>x_0 \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
    Let <m>(x_n)_n</m> be a sequence in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> converging to <m>x_0</m>, and let <m>(r_n)_n</m> be a sequence of positive real numbers converging to <m>r_0</m>.
    Take any <m>y\neq x_0 \pm r_0</m>, then
<md> \lim_{\substack{n \rightarrow \infty \\m \rightarrow \infty}}\chi_{B(x_n,r_m)}(y) = \chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}(y).</md>
Thus the sequence <m>(\chi_{B(x_n,r_m)})_{n,m}</m> converges pointwise a.e. to <m>\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}</m>.
    Hence  <m>(\chi_{B(x_n,r_m)}f)_{n,m}</m> converges pointwise a.e. to <m>\chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}f</m>.
    Further, if <m>r&lt;r_0 + \frac{1}{2}</m> and <m>|x-x_0|&lt;\frac{1}{2}</m> then
<md> |\chi_{B(x,r)}f| \leq \chi_{B(x_0,r_0)}|f|.</md>
It follows now from the <xref ref="thm-LDC" text="custom">Lebesgue Dominated Convergence Theorem</xref> that
<md> (x,r) \mapsto \int_{B(x,r)}f\,dm</md>
is jointly continuous in <m>x</m> and <m>r&gt;0</m>.
    It follows that <m>A_rf(x)</m> is jointly continuous in <m>x</m> and <m>r&gt;0</m>.</p>
</proof>
</lemma>

<corollary xml:id="cor-Hardy-Littlewood">
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>f</m> be an integrable function, then Hardy-Littlewood maximal function <m>Hf</m> is measurable and
        <md>\{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon Hf(x)&gt; \alpha\}</md>
        is open for all <m>\alpha \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
    </p>
</statement>

<proof>
<p>For any <m>\alpha \in \mathbb{R}</m>,
<md> \{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon Hf(x)&gt; \alpha\} = \bigcup_{r&gt;0} \{ x\in \mathbb{R} \colon A_r|f|(x) &gt; \alpha\}.</md>
Since <m>A_r|f|</m> is continuous for all <m>r&gt;0</m>, <m>\{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon Hf(x)&gt; \alpha\}</m> is an open set.
    Thus <m>Hf</m> is measurable.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>

<theorem xml:id="thm-maximal-theorem">
<title>The Hardy-Littlewood Maximal Theorem</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        For all integrable <m>f</m> on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> and <m>\alpha &gt;0</m>,
        <md> m(\{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon Hf(x)&gt; \alpha\}) \leq \frac{3}{\alpha}\int_\mathbb{R} |f|\,dm.</md>
        <idx><h>Hardy-Littlewood Maximal Theorem</h></idx>
    </p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Fix <m>\alpha &gt;0</m> and let <m>E_\alpha = \{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon Hf(x)&gt; \alpha\}</m>.
    By <xref ref="cor-Hardy-Littlewood"/>, <m>E_\alpha</m> is an open set.
    By the continuity of <m>Hf</m>, for each <m>x \in E_\alpha</m> there is an <m>r_x&gt;0</m> such that
<md> A_{r_x}|f|(y) &gt; \alpha</md>
for all <m>y \in B(x,r_x)</m>.
    Note that
<md> E_\alpha = \bigcup_{x\in E_\alpha} B(x,r_x).</md>
Take any <m>c \in \mathbb{R}</m> with <m>0&lt;c &lt; m(E_\alpha)</m>.
    By the inner regularity of Lebesgue measure, <xref ref="thm-Lebesgue-regular"/>, there is a compact set <m>K \subseteq E_\alpha</m> such that
<md> c &lt; m(K) \leq m(E_\alpha).</md>
Note that the sets <m>\{B(x,r_x)\}_{x\in E_\alpha}</m> form and open cover of <m>K</m>, since they are an open cover of <m>E_\alpha</m>.
    As <m>K</m> is compact, there are sets <m>A_1,\ldots,A_m \in \{B(x,r_x)\}_{x\in E_\alpha}</m> such that
<md> K \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^m A_k.</md>
Choose <m>B_1 \in \{A_1,\ldots,A_n\}</m> such that <m>m(B_1)</m> is maximal.
    Choose <m>B_2 \in \{A_1,\ldots,A_n\}</m> such that <m>m(B_2)</m> is maximal in the sets disjoint from <m>B_1</m>.
    Choose <m>B_3 \in \{A_1,\ldots,A_n\}</m> such that <m>m(B_3)</m> is maximal in the sets disjoint from <m>B_1</m> and <m>B_2</m>.
    Proceed in this way to get sets <m>B_1,\ldots,B_n</m> until we exhaust all of the sets in <m>\{A_1,\ldots,A_m\}</m>.
    Let <m>x_j \in E_\alpha</m> be the center point of the interval <m>B_j</m> for <m>1\leq j\leq n</m>.
    For any <m>1\leq i \leq m</m>, there is a <m>1 \leq j \leq n</m>, such that <m>A_i \cap B_j \neq \emptyset.</m>
    Fixing <m>j</m> such that <m>A_i \cap B_j \neq \emptyset</m> and that <m>m(B_j)</m> is maximal.
    Let <m>B_j^*</m> be the open interval with the center <m>x_j</m> as <m>B_j</m> with <m>m(B_j^*) = 3m(B_j).</m>
    Then <m>A_i \subseteq B_j^*</m>.
    Thus <m>B_1^*,\ldots,B_n^*</m> is an open cover of <m>K</m>.
    We thus have
<md>
\begin{aligned}
c &amp;&lt; m(K) \\
        &amp;\leq \sum_{i=1}^n m(B_j^*) \\
        &amp;\leq 3\sum_{i=1}^n m(B_j) \\
        &amp;&lt; \frac{3}{\alpha} \sum_{k=1}^n \int_{B_k}|f|\,dm \\
        &amp;\leq \frac{3}{\alpha}  \int_{\mathbb{R}}|f|\,dm,
\end{aligned}
</md>
where the penultimate inequality comes from the fact that <m>A_{r_{x_j}}|f|(x_j)&gt;\alpha</m>, and the final inequality comes from the fact that <m>B_1,\ldots,B_n</m> are disjoint.
    Taking the supremum over all <m>c&lt;m(E_\alpha)</m> gives the desired inequality.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p>For Fatou's Lemma we needed the concept of a limit inferior, <m>\liminf</m>, for a sequence. For what follows we will need to consider the limit superior of a function. If <m>\varphi</m> is a real-valued function we define the limit superior<idx><h>limit superior</h><h>of a function</h></idx> of <m>\varphi(r)</m> as <m>r</m> approaches <m>R</m> as
<md> \limsup_{r\rightarrow R} \varphi(r) = \lim_{\varepsilon \rightarrow 0} \sup_{0&lt;|r-R|&lt;\varepsilon}\varphi(r)\, \left(= \inf_{\varepsilon&gt;0} \sup_{0&lt;|r-R|&lt;\varepsilon}\varphi(r)\right),</md>
and we define the limit inferior<idx><h>limit inferior</h><h>of a function</h></idx> of <m>\varphi(r)</m> as <m>r</m> approaches <m>R</m> as
<md> \liminf_{r\rightarrow R} \varphi(r) = \lim_{\varepsilon \rightarrow 0} \inf_{0&lt;|r-R|&lt;\varepsilon}\varphi(r)\, \left(= \sup_{\varepsilon&gt;0} \inf_{0&lt;|r-R|&lt;\varepsilon}\varphi(r)\right).</md>
The limit <m>\lim_{r\rightarrow R} \varphi(r) = c</m> if and only if
<md> \limsup_{r\rightarrow R}\varphi(r) =  \liminf_{r\rightarrow R}\varphi(r) =c.</md>
Further, by the definition of the Hardy-Littlewood maximal function,
<md> \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0} |A_rf(x)| \leq Hf(x).</md>
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-lim-avg-f-f">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a Lebesgue integrable function on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, 
            then
            <md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0}A_rf(x) = f(x)</md>
            for almost all <m>x</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
<p>Fix <m>\varepsilon &gt;0</m>.
    By <xref ref="thm-integrable-close-to-cont"/>, there is an integrable continuous function <m>g</m> such that
<md> \int_\mathbb{R} |f-g|\,dm &lt; \varepsilon.</md>
Take <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>\eta&gt;0</m>. 
    By the continuity of <m>g</m> there is an <m>r&gt;0</m>, such that if <m>|x-y|&lt;r</m>, then <m>|g(x) -g(y)|&lt;\eta</m>.
    Thus, if <m>|x-y|&lt;r</m> then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
|A_r g(x) - g(x)| &amp;= \frac{1}{2r}\left|\int_{B(x,r)} (g(y)-g(x))\,dm(y)\right|\\
        &amp;\leq \frac{1}{2r}\int_{B(x,r)} |(g(y)-g(x))|\,dm(y)&lt;\eta.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence, as <m>r\rightarrow 0</m>, <m>A_rg(x) \rightarrow g(x)</m>.</p>
<p>Take any <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>, then
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\left|A_rf(x) - f(x)\right| &amp;= \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\left|A_r(f-g) + (A_rg-g) + (g-f)(x) \right|\\
        &amp;\leq \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}A_r|f-g|(x) + 0 + |g(x)-f(x)| \\
        &amp;\leq H(f-g)(x) + |f-g|(x).
\end{aligned}
</md>
For <m>\alpha &gt;0</m> let
<md>
\begin{aligned}
E_\alpha &amp;= \{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\left|A_rf(x) - f(x)\right|&gt;0 \}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
Then
<md>E_\alpha \subseteq \{x \in \mathbb{R} \colon H(f-g)&gt;\alpha/2\} \cup \{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon |f-g|(x)&gt;\alpha/2\}.</md>
By <xref ref="lem-chenyshev" text="custom">Chebyshev's inequality</xref>, and the choice of <m>g</m>,
<md>m(\{x\in \mathbb{R} \colon |f-g|(x)&gt;\alpha/2\}) \leq \frac{2}{\alpha}\int_\mathbb{R}|f-g|\,dm &lt; \frac{2\varepsilon}{\alpha}. </md>
By the Hardy-Littlewood Maximal Theorem, and the choice of <m>g</m>,
<md> m(\{x \in \mathbb{R} \colon H(f-g)&gt;\alpha/2\}) \leq \frac{6}{\alpha}\int_\mathbb{R}|f-g|\,dm &lt; \frac{6 \varepsilon}{\alpha}. </md>
Thus
<md> m(E_\alpha) \leq \frac{8\varepsilon}{\alpha}.</md>
As this holds for all <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m>, it follows that <m>m(E_\alpha) = 0</m> for all <m>\alpha &gt;0</m>.
</p>
<p>Let <m>E = \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty E_{1/n}</m>.
    Then, <m>m(E) = 0</m>, and if <m>x \notin E</m>,
<md> 0 = \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0} |A_rf(x) -f(x)|.</md>
Thus, <m>\lim_{r\rightarrow 0}A_rf(x) = f(x)</m> for all <m>x \in \mathbb{R} \backslash E</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<p>This brings us now to an important theorem: The Lebesgue Differentiation Theorem. <idx><h>Lebesgue Differentiation Theorem</h></idx></p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-Lebesgue-differentiation">
<title>Lebesgue Differentiation Theorem</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>f</m> be a Lebesgue integrable function on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
        For each <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and <m>r&gt;0</m>, let <m>E_{r^+}(x) = (x,x+r)</m> and <m>E_{r^-}(x) = (x-r,x)</m>.
        Then, for almost all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>,
        <md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{m(E_r(x))} \int_{E_r(x)} |f(y) - f(x)|\,dm(y) = 0</md>
        and
        <md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{m(E_r(x))} \int_{E_r(x)} f\,dm = f(x) </md>
        where <m>E_r = E_{r^+}</m>, <m>E_r = E_{r^-}</m> or <m>E_r = B(x,r)</m>.  
        <idx><h>Lebesgue Differentiation Theorem</h></idx>   
    </p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>Let
<md>L_f = \{x \in \mathbb{R} \colon \lim_{r\rightarrow}\frac{1}{2r}\int_{B(r,x)}|f(y)-f(x)|\,dm(y) =0 \}.</md>
For each <m>c \in \mathbb{R}</m>, let <m>g_c(x) = |f(x) -c|.</m>
    By <xref ref="thm-lim-avg-f-f"/>, there is a Lebesgue measurable set <m>E_c \subseteq \mathbb{R}</m>, such that <m>m(E_c) = 0</m>, and if <m>x \notin E_c</m>, then
<md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} A_rg_c(x) = g_c(x).</md>
Let
<md> E = \bigcup_{q \in \mathbb{Q}} E_q.</md>
Then <m>m(E) = 0</m>, since <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is countable.
    Take <m>x \notin E</m> and <m>\varepsilon &gt;0</m>. 
    By the density of <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, there is a <m>q \in \mathbb{Q}</m> such that
<md> |f(x) - q| &lt; \varepsilon.</md>
For any <m>y \in \mathbb{R}</m>,
<md> |f(y)-f(x)| \leq |f(y)-q| + |f(x)-q| &lt; |f(y)-q| + \varepsilon.</md>
Thus, since <m>x \notin E_q</m>,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\limsup_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{2r} \int_{B(x,r)}|f(y)- f(x)|\,dm(y) &amp;\leq 
        \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{2r} \int_{B(x,r)}(|f(y)- q|+ \varepsilon)\,dm(y)\\
        &amp;=|f(x) - q| + \varepsilon &lt;2\varepsilon.
\end{aligned}
</md>
As <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m> is arbitrary, it follows that <m>x \in L_f</m>. Hence <m>m(\mathbb{R}\backslash L_f) =0</m>.</p>
<p>Now, for all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>E_r = E_{r^+}</m> or <m>E_r = E_{r^-}</m>
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{r}\int_{E_r}|f(y)-f(x)|\,dm(y) 
        &amp;\leq \frac{1}{r}\int_{B(x,r)}|f(y)-f(x)|\,dm(y) \\
        &amp;\leq 2\frac{1}{2r}\int_{B(x,r)}|f(y)-f(x)|\,dm(y).
\end{aligned}
</md>
Thus the first equality holds for all <m>x \in L_f</m>.
    The second equality follows from <xref ref="thm-lim-avg-f-f"/>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<theorem xml:id="thm-Borel-radon-nikodym-derivative">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>\nu</m> be a Borel signed-measure on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
            Let <m>f = \frac{d\nu}{dm}</m> be the Radon-Nikodym derivative of <m>\nu</m> with respect to the Lebesgue measure <m>m</m>.
            Then,
            <md>f(x) =  \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu((x,x+r))}{r}  =\lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu((x-r,x))}{r} = \lim_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\nu((x-r,x+r)}{2r}, </md>
            for almost all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>.        
        </p>
    </statement>
<proof>
<p>Note that
<md> \left|\frac{\nu((x,x+r))}{r}\right| \leq \frac{|\nu|((x,x+r))}{r}\leq \frac{|\nu|((x-r,x+r))}{r}= 2 \frac{|\nu|(B(x,r))}{2r}.</md>
Similarly
<md> \left|\frac{\nu((x-r,x))}{r}\right| \leq \frac{|\nu|(B(x,r))}{2r}.</md>
Thus, to prove the result we can assume <m>\nu</m> is a measure, and we need only need to show that
<md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu(B(r,x))}{2r}=f(x)</md>
for almost all <m>x</m>.</p>
<p>Assume first that <m>\nu \perp m</m>.
    In this case <m>f = 0</m>.
    By the assumption that <m>\nu\perp m</m>, there are Borel sets <m>A</m> and <m>B=A^c</m> so that <m>\mathbb{R} = A \cup B</m> and <m>\nu(A) = m(B) = 0</m>.
    For each <m>k</m>, let
<md> F_k = \left\{x \in A \colon \limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\nu(B(x,r))}{2r}&gt;\frac{1}{k}\right\}.</md>
Fix <m>k</m>. We will show that <m>m(F_k) =0</m>. The argument is similar to that in the Hardy-Littlewood Maximal Theorem, <xref ref="thm-maximal-theorem"/>.</p>
<p>Since <m>\nu</m> is outer regular, <xref ref="lem-regular-borel"/>, for each <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m> there is an open set <m>U_\varepsilon \supseteq A</m>, such that <m>\nu(U_\varepsilon)&lt;\varepsilon.</m>
    For each <m>x \in U_\varepsilon</m>, there is a <m>r_x&gt;0</m>, so that <m>B(x,r_x) \subseteq U_\varepsilon.</m>
    Arguing as in <xref ref="thm-maximal-theorem"/>, if <m>c &lt; m\left(\bigcup_{x\in F_k} B(x,r_x)\right)</m> then there is a finite collection <m>x_1,\ldots,x_n \in F_k</m> so that <m>\{B(x_i,r_{x_i})\}_{i=1}^n</m> are disjoint and
<md> c &lt; 3 \sum_{i=1}^n m(B(x_i,r_{x_i}))\leq \sum 3k\sum_{i=1}^n \nu(B(x_i,r_{x_i}))\leq 3k\varepsilon.</md>
Taking the supremum over all <m>c \leq m(F_k)</m> we get <m>m(F_k)\leq 3k\varepsilon</m> for all <m>\varepsilon &gt;0</m>.
    Hence <m>m(F_k) = 0</m>.
    Let <m>F = \bigcup_k F_k</m>.
    Then <m>m(F) = 0</m>, and if <m>x \notin F</m>
<md>\limsup_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\nu(B(x,r))}{2r} =0</md>
and hence
<md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0}\frac{\nu(B(x,r))}{2r} =0.</md>
Now let <m>\nu</m> be an arbitrary Borel measure on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
    Applying the <xref ref="thm-lebesegue-decomposition" text="custom">Lebesgue Decomposition Theorem</xref> to <m>\nu</m>, we have <m>\nu = \nu_a + \nu_s</m> where <m>\nu_a \ll m</m> and <m>\nu_s \perp m</m>.
    In this case
<md> \frac{d\nu}{dm} = \frac{d\nu_a}{dm} = f.</md>
By the <xref ref="thm-Lebesgue-differentiation" text="custom">Lebesgue Differentiation Theorem</xref> and the <xref ref="thm-Radon-Nikodym" text="custom">Radon-Nikodym Theorem</xref>,
<md>
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu_a(B(x,r))}{2r}  = \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{2r}\int_{B(x,r)}f\,dm = f(x).
\end{aligned}
</md>
Hence
<md> \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu(B(x,r))}{2r} = \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu_a(B(x,r))}{2r} + \lim_{r\rightarrow 0} \frac{\nu_s(B(x,r))}{2r}
    = f(x) + 0 = f(x)</md>
for almost all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>

<corollary xml:id="cor-f-integrable">
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>f</m> be a right-continuous function of bounded variation on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m> and let <m>\nu= \nu_f</m> be the signed Borel-measure on <m>[a,b]</m> extending the premeasure <m>\nu((c,d]) = f(d) - f(c)</m> and <m>\nu(\{a\}) = 0</m>.
        Then <m>f'</m> is defined for almost all <m>x \in [a,b]</m> and <m>f'</m> is integrable.     
    </p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>If <m>h \neq 0</m> then>
<md> f(x+h) - f(x) = \begin{cases}\nu((x,x+h]) &amp; \text{if }h&gt;0 \\
                                    -\nu((x+h,x])&amp; \text{if }h&lt;0.\end{cases}</md>
It follows from <xref ref="thm-Borel-radon-nikodym-derivative"/>
<md> f'(x) = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}=\frac{d\nu}{dm}(x)</md>
for a.a. <m>x \in [a,b]</m>.
    As the Radon-Nikodym derivative <m>\frac{d\nu}{dm}</m> is integrable, <m>f'</m> is integrable.</p>
</proof>
</corollary>

<theorem xml:id="thm-Lebesgue-FToC">
<title>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus for Lebesgue Integrals</title>
<statement>
    <p>
        Let <m>f</m> be a function on a closed interval <m>[a,b]</m>. 
        Then the following are equivalent
        <ol>
        <li><m>f</m> is absolutely continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>;
        </li>
        <li>for <m>x \in [a,b]</m>, <m>f(x) - f(a) = \int_{[a,x]} h\,dm</m> for some integrable function <m>h</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>;
        </li>
        <li><m>f</m> is differentiable almost everywhere on <m>[a,b]</m>, <m>f'</m> is integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>, and for <m>x \in [a,b]</m> 
                <md> f(x) - f(a) = \int_{[a,x]}f'\,dm.</md>
        </li>
        </ol>
        <idx><h>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</h><h>Lebesgue integration</h></idx>
    </p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>By replacing <m>f</m> with <m>f - f(a)</m> we can assume <m>f(a) = 0</m>.
    Assume <m>f</m> is absolutely continuous. 
    By <xref ref="prop-abs-cont-jordan-decomp"/>, <m>f</m> is of bounded variation.
    Thus <m>f'</m> exists a.e. and <m>f'</m> is integrable by <xref ref="cor-f-integrable"/>.
    Let <m>\nu</m> be the signed-Borel measure such that <m>f</m> is the cumulative distribution function for <m>\nu</m>.
    Then, by <xref ref="prop-abs-cont-meas-and-func"/>, <m>\nu</m> is absolutely continuous.
    Thus, by <xref ref="thm-Borel-radon-nikodym-derivative"/>
<md> f(x) = \nu([a,x]) = \int_{[a,x]}f'\,dm.</md>
Hence (i) implies (iii).
    That (iii) implies (ii) is clear by setting <m>h = f'</m>.</p>
<p>Finally, suppose (ii) holds.
    Let <m>h</m> be an integrable function on <m>[a,b]</m> such that
<md> f(x) = \int_{[a,x]}h\,dm</md>
for <m>x\in[a,b]</m>.
    Let <m>\nu</m> be the Borel signed-measure
<md> \nu(E) = \int_E h\,dm</md>
for Borel sets <m>E \subseteq[a,b]</m>.
    Then <m>\nu</m> is absolutely continuous, and
<md> f(x) = \nu([a,x]).</md>
By <xref ref="prop-abs-cont-meas-and-func"/>, <m>f</m> is absolutely continuous.</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
</section>